<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=double-entry-bookkeeping">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Double-entry bookkeeping</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1211">1211</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as double-entry accounting</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Double-entry bookkeeping records every financial transaction with equal and opposite entries, debits and credits, so that the books balance. One of the earliest known records is a ledger kept in 1211 by Florentine money changers at a fair in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, which shows a fairly developed use of debit and credit. The 1299–1300 ledger of the Farolfi firm, kept by Amatino Manucci, shows full double-entry. The system spread between Italian merchant cities in the 14th century, and Luca Pacioli first codified it in his Summa de arithmetica, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1494. It reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-england">Bank of England</a> used it.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A commercial treatise of 1458 by the Ragusan Benedikt Kotruljević contains one of the earliest descriptions of <b>double-entry bookkeeping</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Republic of Ragusa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Venetian commercial practice, including partnership contracts, state-regulated shipping, <b>double-entry bookkeeping</b> and the public debt, influenced business across Europe, and the city produced travellers such as Marco Polo, whose account of Asia was widely read.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Leonardo Fibonacci’s Liber Abaci (1202) taught merchants Hindu-Arabic arithmetic, Italian merchants developed <b>double-entry bookkeeping</b>, set out in print by Luca Pacioli in 1494, and Florentine humanists such as Leonardo Bruni praised their republic’s liberty as the source of its greatness, an outlook the historian Hans Baron called civic…</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Double-entry bookkeeping</b> made the inflow and outflow of trade plain and so drew attention to the balance of trade.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/mercantilism">Mercantilism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1211</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Bologna, Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Amatino Manucci · Luca Pacioli</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The 1211 ledger may imply the system began to evolve in the 12th century. Some sources suggest Giovanni di Bicci de' Medici introduced it for the Medici bank in the 14th century, though evidence for this is lacking.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=double-entry-bookkeeping">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
